\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A constant flux on a torus}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{F=\mathbb C}, let #{w} be an integer with #{|w|<N_1N_2/2}, and put
#{\varphi=2\pi w/(N_1N_2)}. With coordinates #{0\le x_j<N_j}, let
#{U_2(x)=\ue^{\ui\varphi x_1}}, let #{U_1(x)=\ue^{-2\pi\ui wx_2/N_2}} when #{x_1=N_1-1} and
#{U_1(x)=1} otherwise, and let #{U_j(x)=1} for #{j\ge 3}. Then #{u_p=\ue^{\ui\varphi}} for
every plaquette #{p=(x;1,2)}. When #{x_1<N_1-1}, the two links in direction #{1} are trivial and the
phase difference in direction #{2} is #{\varphi}. On the last row
#{x_1=N_1-1} the latter difference is #{-(N_1-1)\varphi}. The two links in
direction #{1} contribute #{2\pi w/N_2}, with an extra #{-2\pi w} when
#{x_2=N_2-1}. At the double seam #{x_1=N_1-1}, #{x_2=N_2-1},
the oriented sum of the displayed link phases is #{\varphi -2\pi w}. Elsewhere in
this plane it is #{\varphi}. Both give the same unit complex number.
Every plaquette in another plane has #{u_p=1}. All
plaquettes are plain, since #{|\varphi|<\pi}. For fixed #{x_3,\ldots,x_n} the sum #{T} of the
#{N_1N_2} plaquettes #{(x;1,2)} is a [closed plaquette surface](fdip-015V), because its
links cancel in pairs by periodicity, and #{\operatorname{fl}(T)=\frac{N_1N_2\varphi}{2\pi}=w}.}
